Two guards in the fabrication-gate layer, both from measurements taken
during tonight's health checks.

REQ-CONDUCTOR-RECEIPT-2 — a caller timeout must outlast its own budget.
REQ-CONDUCTOR-RECEIPT-1 gave the QA-layer audit a budget it knows about so
it could write a PARTIAL report instead of dying. It still died, because
the two numbers were set independently and contradicted each other: budget
750s, kill timeout 900s, slack 150s, measured cost of one unit 250-375s.
The budget is checked BETWEEN units, never inside one, so a unit starting
near the deadline runs past the kill and no report lands at all. Effect:
two PARTIAL reports in a month, of 2 and 3 units against --limit 20, a hard
timeout at the 2026-08-22T22:33Z close, and rotation stuck at offset 5 of
174 units.

AUDIT_TIMEOUT_SLACK_S = 600 states the relationship. QA-layer budget 750 ->
1800s and timeout 900 -> 2400s; the claim audit keeps its 750s budget and
gets timeout 900 -> 1350s, which had the same too-small-slack shape.
test_audit_run_receipts.py::TestBudgetTimeoutSlack AST-parses the real call
sites and asserts the inequality, so the numbers cannot drift apart again.
Mutations: revert the QA timeout -> RED; shrink the slack constant -> RED;
revert the QA budget -> RED; each restored byte-identical, GREEN.

REQ-SUBSTRATE-ALIAS-1 — a new alias carries evidence, not only a name.
adversarial_verify.py skips DURATION_TOO_SHORT for any substrate named in
NO_LLM_SUBSTRATE_ALIASES, and nothing governed who may add a name. Measured
2026-08-23: 38 aliases, 19 added in two days, and every sampled addition
landed in the same conductor commit as the artifact it exempted — 59c8f8602d
registered an alias at 22:24Z and wrote experiment_6520 at 22:25Z. So an
experiment could clear the gate by naming its own substrate in the gate.
That is the QA-Layer Authenticity Discipline's founding concern in live
form, and the inverse of the pattern-list-narrower-than-its-concept bug:
here the list widens to accommodate what it was built to catch.

scripts/substrate_alias_evidence_lint.py refuses a commit that adds an
alias without either a test naming the string or a dated line in
ops/substrate_alias_acks.md. Fails closed when the diff cannot be read.
Removals and unrelated edits pass — the requirement is on widening.
Verified end to end against the exp6520 shape: staging a real alias
addition refuses with the alias named; adding an ack line passes.

Mutations: evidence check disabled -> 5 RED; fail-open on git error -> RED;
drop the HEAD subtraction -> RED. Dropping the '+'-prefix filter first left
the suite GREEN — the check was decorative, double-covered by the HEAD
subtraction. Rather than delete it, added a test pinning the case where it
does bite (empty or stale head_text, where every '-' line would parse as an
addition and REMOVING an alias would demand evidence). It is RED now.

The new lint is itself a guard, so qa-layer-audit-scope-check refused this
commit until it was classified. It is now in GUARD_TARGETS with its reason:
a silent non-firing there lets an experiment clear the fabrication gate by
naming its own substrate. That scope check working on this very commit is
the machinery behaving as designed.

The 38 aliases present today are grandfathered; auditing them is separate,
still-open work noted in the ack file.

